description:
## What to build

Live prompting through the server: server-owned Turns with buffer + replay, WebSocket event streaming, one active Turn per graph, and the graph-blind turn journal with client apply-on-reattach (PRD: docs/prd/mobile-push.md, M4; CONTEXT.md: Turn, Attachment). Decision context: the server never parses `.thoughttree` content — a reattaching client applies completed turns via GraphModel and acks (grilled decision; keeps the server graph-agnostic).

Turn registry (in `thoughttree-server`):

```rust
pub struct TurnId(pub String);              // uuid v4

pub enum TurnState { Streaming, AwaitingPermission, Completed, Failed }

pub struct TurnRegistry { /* Mutex<HashMap<GraphPath, ActiveTurn>> + journal handle */ }

pub struct ActiveTurn {
    pub turn_id: TurnId,
    pub node_id: String,
    pub state: TurnState,
    pub chunks: Vec<StreamChunkEvent>,                        // replay buffer, drained to journal on completion
    pub events_tx: tokio::sync::broadcast::Sender<ServerEvent>,
}

#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]
#[error("graph already has an active turn: {0}")]
pub struct TurnBusy(pub TurnId);

impl TurnRegistry {
    pub fn begin(&self, graph_path: &str, node_id: &str) -> Result<TurnHandle, TurnBusy>;
    /// Snapshot for a newly attached client + live receiver. Snapshot includes the active
    /// turn's buffered chunks (if any) and all unacked completed turns from the journal.
    pub fn subscribe(&self, graph_path: &str) -> (AttachSnapshot, tokio::sync::broadcast::Receiver<ServerEvent>);
}
```

`ServerEventSink` implements core's `SessionEventSink`: each `StreamChunkEvent` is appended to the buffer and broadcast. The ACP session itself runs exactly like desktop (core's LocalSet runtime helper); the server spawns it detached from any connection — a dropped WebSocket changes nothing for the Turn.

Turn journal — durability for detached completions: append-only JSONL in `TT_DATA_DIR/turns.jsonl`, one record per completed/failed turn:

```json
{ "turnId": "...", "graphPath": "...", "nodeId": "...", "state": "completed",
  "finalContent": "...", "provider": "...", "modelId": "...", "completedEpochMs": 0, "acked": false }
```

Unacked records are included in every `AttachSnapshot` for that graph; acked records are dropped on startup compaction. Journal is graph-blind: it stores the transcript, never touches `.thoughttree` files.

Protocol — actions over HTTP, events over WebSocket (simple reconnects, no client→server WS messages in v1):

```
POST /api/graphs/{*path}/prompt   body { nodeId, messages, provider?, modelId?, effort? }
                                  → 202 { "turnId": "..." } | 409 { "activeTurnId": "..." }
POST /api/turns/{turnId}/ack      → 200            (client applied + saved the turn output)
GET  /api/graphs/{*path}/events   → WebSocket upgrade
  on connect, server sends:
    { "type": "snapshot", "activeTurn": { turnId, nodeId, state, chunks: [...] } | null,
      "unackedTurns": [ <journal records> ], "parkedPermissions": [] }
  then live events:
    { "type": "stream-chunk", "turnId": "...", ...StreamChunkEvent }
    { "type": "turn-finished", "turnId": "...", "nodeId": "...", "state": "completed"|"failed", "finalContent": "..." }
    { "type": "permission-request", ... }   (wired fully in the parked-permissions issue)
```

The `messages` array is the Conversation path built client-side by GraphModel — same contract as the desktop `send_prompt` command.

Frontend (`HttpTransport` + store): `sendPrompt` → POST prompt + ensure WS subscription for the open graph; WS `stream-chunk` events feed the existing `onStreamChunk` path unchanged. Reattach flow on `snapshot`: for each unacked turn — apply `finalContent` to the target node via GraphModel, guarded-save the Project file, then POST ack; if a turn is actively streaming, replay its chunks into the node then continue live. A prompt attempt while a Turn is active surfaces the 409 in the UI ("a response is already streaming in this graph").

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Prompt from the browser streams into the canvas via WS, matching desktop behavior
- [ ] Kill the tab mid-stream: server completes the Turn (verify via log/journal); reopen → snapshot replays, node filled, file guarded-saved, turn acked
- [ ] Restart the server after a detached completion: unacked turn survives (journal) and is applied on next attach
- [ ] Second prompt on the same graph during an active Turn → 409, surfaced in UI; prompt on a different graph works concurrently
- [ ] Startup compaction drops acked journal records
- [ ] Rust tests: TurnRegistry begin/busy/subscribe-replay semantics; journal write/compact/unacked-selection. Frontend test: reattach apply+ack flow with a mocked transport

## Blocked by

- Server skeleton (projects API + HttpTransport)

## What to build

Implement Guarded writes (ADR 0004: docs/adr/0004-guarded-project-writes.md; CONTEXT.md: Guarded write, Vault, Project file) in `thoughttree-core` and migrate the desktop save path to them, end-to-end through to a stale-save UX in the frontend. Today's save is a bare `std::fs::write` — last writer wins silently.

Storage abstraction in core (`thoughttree-core/src/vault/`):

```rust
/// Opaque optimistic-concurrency token. LocalFsVault: SHA-256 hex of file content.
/// WebDAV backend (later issue): ETag. Callers never interpret it.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize)]
pub struct Revision(pub String);

pub struct ProjectDoc { pub content: String, pub revision: Revision }
pub struct ProjectEntry { pub relative_path: String, pub modified_epoch_ms: u64 }

#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]
pub enum VaultError {
    #[error("project not found")] NotFound,
    #[error("stale revision: file changed since read")] Stale { current: Revision },
    #[error("path escapes vault root")] InvalidPath,
    #[error(transparent)] Io(#[from] std::io::Error),
}

#[async_trait::async_trait]
pub trait VaultStorage: Send + Sync {
    async fn list(&self) -> Result<Vec<ProjectEntry>, VaultError>;   // *.thoughttree under root
    async fn read(&self, relative_path: &str) -> Result<ProjectDoc, VaultError>;
    /// expected = None → unconditional write (new-file / explicit overwrite path).
    /// expected = Some → guarded: succeeds iff current content still hashes to expected.
    async fn write(&self, relative_path: &str, content: &str, expected: Option<&Revision>)
        -> Result<Revision, VaultError>;
}

pub struct LocalFsVault { root: PathBuf }
```

`LocalFsVault::write` mechanics: read current file → compare hash to `expected` → write temp file in the same directory → atomic rename over target. The small check-then-rename window on local FS is accepted (single desktop process per synced copy; cross-device races remain Nextcloud's residual — ADR 0004). Path validation ports the existing `save_project` validation (security: reject traversal outside root; keep or strengthen current checks).

Desktop wiring: the desktop saves via absolute paths from native dialogs, which may point outside the notes directory — so expose the guarded write mechanics as a core function usable for a bare absolute path as well (e.g. `guarded_write_file(path, content, expected)`), with `LocalFsVault` building on it. Tauri commands change: `load_project` returns `{ content, revision }`; `save_project` accepts `base_revision: Option<String>` and maps `VaultError::Stale` to a typed error the frontend can distinguish.

Frontend: `TauriTransport.saveProject` now plumbs real revisions (interface already final from the transport-seam issue). Store tracks the current revision per open Project file, updates it on every successful save/load. On `StaleRevisionError`: dialog with two actions — Reload (discard local changes, re-fetch) or Overwrite (force-save with `baseRevision: null`). Minimal but honest v1 conflict UX.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `VaultStorage` + `LocalFsVault` in core with unit tests: guarded write succeeds on matching revision; stale write returns `Stale { current }` and leaves file untouched; write is temp+rename (no partial content observable); traversal path rejected
- [ ] Desktop save/load use guarded writes; revision round-trips through the transport
- [ ] Stale-save dialog works: two app instances (or manual file edit while open) → second save rejected → Reload and Overwrite both behave as specified
- [ ] `cargo test --workspace`, `bun test:run`, clippy clean
- [ ] ADR 0004 referenced from the code where the CAS decision is implemented (module doc comment)

## Blocked by

- Frontend transport seam (BackendTransport)
- Cargo workspace split (thoughttree-core)

## What to build

Decouple the Rust ACP layer from Tauri so it can later move into `thoughttree-core` and be driven by an axum server (PRD: docs/prd/mobile-push.md, M2 prerequisite). Today `StreamingClient` holds a `tauri::AppHandle` and emits `stream-chunk` / `permission-request` events directly, and pending permissions live as a raw `HashMap<String, oneshot::Sender<String>>` in `AppState`. Replace both couplings with two backend-owned abstractions; behavior and event payloads stay identical.

Event sink — the only channel through which sessions talk to the outside:

```rust
// backend/events.rs
#[derive(Clone, serde::Serialize)]
pub struct StreamChunkEvent { pub node_id: String, /* carry over the exact fields emitted today */ }

#[derive(Clone, serde::Serialize)]
pub struct PermissionRequestEvent {
    pub request_id: String,
    pub node_id: String,
    /* carry over the exact fields of today's "permission-request" payload (tool name, options with ids/labels) */
}

/// Implementations must be cheap and non-blocking (called from the LocalSet thread mid-stream).
/// Not required to be Send — ACP futures are ?Send (see CONTEXT.md: LocalSet runtime).
pub trait SessionEventSink: Clone + 'static {
    fn stream_chunk(&self, event: StreamChunkEvent);
    fn permission_request(&self, event: PermissionRequestEvent);
}
```

Permission broker — owns the park/answer lifecycle (CONTEXT.md: Permission channel, Parked permission):

```rust
// backend/permissions.rs
pub struct PermissionBroker { inner: Arc<tokio::sync::Mutex<BrokerInner>> }

struct BrokerInner { pending: HashMap<String, PendingPermission> }
struct PendingPermission { request: PermissionRequestEvent, responder: oneshot::Sender<String> }

#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]
pub enum RespondError { #[error("no pending permission request with id {0}")] UnknownRequest(String) }

impl PermissionBroker {
    pub fn new() -> Self;
    /// Registers the request, emits it via the sink, then awaits the user's answer. Returns chosen option id.
    /// No timeout by design (ADR 0004 context; PRD "park, no timeout").
    pub async fn request(&self, request: PermissionRequestEvent, sink: &impl SessionEventSink) -> anyhow::Result<String>;
    pub async fn respond(&self, request_id: &str, option_id: String) -> Result<(), RespondError>;
    /// Snapshot of parked requests — the server will surface these on client reattach.
    pub async fn pending(&self) -> Vec<PermissionRequestEvent>;
}
```

Wiring: `PromptSessionParams.app_handle` is replaced by a generic `sink: S where S: SessionEventSink`; `pending_permissions` is replaced by `broker: PermissionBroker` (stored in `AppState`). The Tauri crate provides `TauriEventSink(AppHandle)` emitting the same event names/payloads as today. `respond_to_permission` command delegates to `broker.respond`. `run_summary_session` / `run_model_discovery_session` get the same treatment where they touch `AppHandle`.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `backend/acp/clients.rs` and `backend/acp/sessions.rs` have no `tauri` imports; all outward communication goes through `SessionEventSink`
- [ ] `TauriEventSink` emits byte-identical event names and payload shapes ("stream-chunk", "permission-request") — frontend untouched
- [ ] `PermissionBroker` replaces the raw pending-permissions map; `respond_to_permission` delegates to it
- [ ] Unit tests without Tauri: a fake sink collects `StreamChunkEvent`s; permission round-trip (request → pending() shows it → respond → request() resolves with option id; respond to unknown id → `UnknownRequest`)
- [ ] `cargo fmt` / `cargo clippy --lib -- -D warnings` / `cargo test --lib` pass; desktop smoke (prompt + permission flow) unchanged

## Blocked by

None - can start immediately.

## What to build

Introduce a frontend transport seam so the UI can run against either the Tauri IPC backend (desktop, shape A) or an HTTP/WebSocket backend (shape C PWA, later issues). Today `invoke()` and Tauri `listen()` are called directly from the Zustand store and components; after this issue, all backend communication that the PWA will need goes through one interface with a Tauri implementation (PRD: docs/prd/mobile-push.md; CONTEXT.md: Deployment shape).

Design (decision-rich shape; adjust field details to match existing payloads exactly):

```ts
// src/lib/transport/types.ts
export interface ProjectDoc { data: string; revision: string }
export interface ProjectEntry { relativePath: string; modifiedEpochMs: number }

export class StaleRevisionError extends Error {
  constructor(public currentRevision: string) { super('project file changed since last read') }
}

export type Unsubscribe = () => void

export interface BackendTransport {
  // Project files (Vault)
  loadProject(path: string): Promise<ProjectDoc>
  /** Guarded write. baseRevision null = unconditional (new file). Throws StaleRevisionError. Returns new revision. */
  saveProject(path: string, data: string, baseRevision: string | null): Promise<string>
  listProjects(): Promise<ProjectEntry[]>

  // Chat / ACP
  sendPrompt(req: PromptRequest): Promise<string>  // resolves with final assistant text, mirrors send_prompt command
  respondToPermission(requestId: string, optionId: string): Promise<void>
  // mirror the remaining PWA-relevant invokes: model discovery, summary, effort — same shapes as existing commands

  // Events (today: Tauri events "stream-chunk" / "permission-request")
  onStreamChunk(cb: (ev: StreamChunk) => void): Unsubscribe
  onPermissionRequest(cb: (ev: PermissionRequest) => void): Unsubscribe

  /** Feature detection instead of platform sniffing in components. */
  readonly capabilities: { nativeDialogs: boolean }
}
```

`TauriTransport` implements this by wrapping the existing `invoke()` calls and `listen()` subscriptions 1:1. Revision plumbing: until guarded writes land (guarded-writes issue), `TauriTransport.saveProject` ignores `baseRevision` and returns a dummy revision — the interface is final now so call sites don't churn twice.

Scope boundary: desktop-only surfaces (native open/new/pick dialogs, notes-directory picking, recent projects, config store access) stay as direct Tauri calls in a clearly separated module, guarded by `capabilities.nativeDialogs` at the call sites that branch. The transport covers exactly what the PWA needs.

Transport selection: single module-level provider (or React context) initialized at boot; store actions receive/import the transport, never `invoke` directly.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `BackendTransport` interface + `TauriTransport` implementation exist; store and chat/permission components consume the transport only
- [ ] `rg "invoke\(" src/` hits only `TauriTransport` and the designated desktop-only module (dialogs/config)
- [ ] Tauri event names and payloads unchanged ("stream-chunk", "permission-request") — Rust side untouched
- [ ] Existing tests pass; store tests mock `BackendTransport` instead of `invoke` where they previously mocked IPC
- [ ] Desktop app behavior unchanged (`bun run tauri dev` smoke: load, save, prompt, permission)

## Blocked by

None - can start immediately.
